WEBVTT

1
00:00:00.500 --> 00:00:06.020
In a Go web service, trust boundaries appear wherever data enters through net/http.

2
00:00:06.120 --> 00:00:19.320
Request bodies, query parameters, route parameters, headers, cookies, JSON payloads, form data, and multipart file uploads all arrive from outside the trusted application core.

3
00:00:19.420 --> 00:00:25.100
A router or framework may help extract those values, but extraction is not validation.

4
00:00:25.200 --> 00:00:32.160
The program still needs to decide whether each value is present, well formed, within limits, and appropriate for the current action.

5
00:00:32.440 --> 00:00:35.960
Command-line tools and services have quieter boundaries.

6
00:00:36.060 --> 00:00:41.180
Command-line arguments may point to files, choose modes, or set operational limits.

7
00:00:41.280 --> 00:00:52.640
Environment variables and configuration files may be missing, use an old name, contain a value from the wrong environment, or hold a string where the application expects a number, duration, or URL.

8
00:00:52.740 --> 00:00:59.780
These values deserve the same care as request data because they can change program behavior before any user request arrives.

9
00:01:00.060 --> 00:01:03.020
Distributed systems add more sources of uncertainty.

10
00:01:03.120 --> 00:01:08.880
Message queues can deliver duplicate, delayed, malformed, or version-skewed messages.

11
00:01:08.980 --> 00:01:13.620
Database records may contain legacy states that no longer match current assumptions.

12
00:01:13.720 --> 00:01:20.920
External API responses can omit fields, change formats, or return values outside documented ranges.

13
00:01:21.020 --> 00:01:27.260
Treating every internal-looking value as trusted is how validation gaps move deeper into the codebase.

14
00:01:27.540 --> 00:01:30.420
A practical Go design names its boundaries.

15
00:01:30.520 --> 00:01:39.960
The handler, consumer, CLI entry point, or configuration loader should accept raw data, validate it, and pass a smaller trusted representation to the rest of the application.

16
00:01:40.060 --> 00:01:45.020
That pattern reduces defensive clutter in business logic and makes the code easier to test.

17
00:01:45.120 --> 00:01:51.920
Reviewers can ask a simple question: where does unknown data enter the program, and where is it converted into trusted input?
